# 6. MAKE EVERY DROP FUNCTIONAL

Create a central drag-and-drop action engine.

Use a consistent action architecture with the following logical flow:

DRAG START
↓
IDENTIFY CARD TYPE AND ID
↓
HIGHLIGHT COMPATIBLE ACTION ZONES
↓
USER DROPS CARD
↓
VALIDATE CARD, ZONE AND PERMISSIONS
↓
COLLECT REQUIRED INFORMATION
↓
VALIDATE BUSINESS RULES
↓
EXECUTE AUTHORIZED BACKEND OPERATION
↓
UPDATE DATABASE AND UI
↓
DISPLAY RESULT AND AUDIT EVENT

Implement reusable functions similar to:

* handleDragStart(card)
* handleDragOver(zone)
* handleDrop(card, zone)
* validateDrop(card, zone, user)
* executeCanvasAction(card, zone, user)
* refreshCanvasData()
* showActionFeedback(result)

Adapt these names to the existing codebase rather than duplicating existing business logic.

### Important engineering rules

* Never trust client-side permissions.
* Enforce authorization on the server.
* Validate all required fields.
* Prevent duplicate report submissions.
* Prevent duplicate claims where prohibited.
* Handle failed network requests.
* Avoid silent failures.
* Prevent incompatible card types from triggering actions.
* Prevent accidental deletion or handover.
* Update persistent data only after successful validation.
* Use transactions where multiple related records must change together.
* Ensure that retrying a failed operation does not create duplicate records.
* Preserve data integrity during concurrent actions.

A successful animation is not proof that an operation succeeded. Show success only after the backend confirms the result.

